{
  "id": "measurement_of_electrical_power",
  "title": "Measurement of Electrical Power",
  "description": "Measure electric power with an ammeter and a voltmeter. Calculate power using P = U × I or P = R × I²",
  "type": "chart-table",
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "# Measurement of Electrical Power\n\nElectric power in a circuit can be measured with an ammeter (amperemeter) and a voltmeter.\n\nThe power can be calculated as:\n\n**P = U × I** or **P = R × I²**\n\nWhere:\n- **P** = electric power (W, watts)\n- **U** = voltage (V, volt)\n- **I** = current (A, ampere)\n- **R** = resistance (Ω, ohm)\n\n### Example\nFor a 230 V household circuit with an electric heater measuring 8 A:\n\nP = (230 V) × (8 A) = 1,840 W"
    },
    {
      "type": "calculator",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "voltage",
          "label": "Voltage (U)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "V",
          "defaultValue": 230
        },
        {
          "id": "current",
          "label": "Current (I)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "A",
          "defaultValue": 8
        },
        {
          "id": "resistance",
          "label": "Resistance (R)",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "Ω",
          "defaultValue": 0
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "id": "power_ui",
          "label": "Power (P = U × I)",
          "formula": "inputs.voltage * inputs.current",
          "unit": "W",
          "format": "decimal"
        },
        {
          "id": "power_ri2",
          "label": "Power (P = R × I²)",
          "formula": "inputs.resistance * inputs.current * inputs.current",
          "unit": "W",
          "format": "decimal"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "datatable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Symbol",
        "Unit",
        "Value"
      ],
      "rows": [
